\section*{Integral scalar-curvature control of simplicial volume}

\subsection{OpenAI's claim}
\textit{Integral negative scalar curvature}: For each $n\ge3$, the manuscript claims a dimension-only positive lower bound on the integral of $(\mathrm{Scal}^-)^{n/2}$ in terms of real simplicial volume. The bound is uniform over closed oriented manifolds and their metrics.

\subsection{Mathematical statement of the claimed result}
\paragraph{Mathematical claim 1.} For each integer $n\ge3$, some dimension-dependent $a_n>0$ is claimed to satisfy \[\int_M\bigl(\max\{0,-\operatorname{Scal}_g\}\bigr)^{n/2}\,dV_g\ge a_n\|M\|\] for each smooth metric $g$ on a closed smooth $n$-manifold $M$ which is oriented and connected. Here $\|M\|$ is the infimum of the sum of absolute coefficients over finite real singular cycles representing its fundamental class. Thus nonnegative scalar curvature forces $\|M\|=0$, but this implication alone does not treat all aspherical manifolds with vanishing simplicial volume.

\subsection{Outline of the argument}
\textit{Integral negative scalar curvature}: The companion vanishing result forces a negative Yamabe minimizer, which is normalized to scalar curvature minus one; Hölder then reduces the desired integral estimate to a volume bound. A bounded cocycle is represented through an equivariant probability map from the cover. Weighted graph deformations increase map energy while retaining the volume budget, and a coordinate-by-coordinate degree estimate pays for the detecting cocycle, yielding the uniform comparison.

\subsection{Scope relative to this problem}
\textit{Integral negative scalar curvature}: For each $n\ge3$, the manuscript claims a dimension-only positive lower bound on the integral of $(\mathrm{Scal}^-)^{n/2}$ in terms of real simplicial volume. The bound is uniform over closed oriented manifolds and their metrics. The parameters, regularity assumptions and normalization in this question are retained in applying that assertion.

With the hypotheses and conventions above, the claimed result addresses the whole mathematical assertion on this page.

